/* ============================================================================
   euler-identity-check.epher -- exp(i pi) + 1 is zero within 1e-12

   Euler's identity e^(i pi) = -1 (Leonhard Euler, 1748, "Introductio in
   analysin infinitorum") is the special case x = pi of Euler's formula
   e^(ix) = cos x + i sin x. exp accepts complex arguments here, so both
   identities are checked directly. The imaginary residue of e^(i pi) is
   floating-point noise of about 1e-16, so the transcript prints the
   magnitude checks, which come out true, and the exact real part -1.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/algebra/complex/euler-identity-check.epher"

   No knobs to edit; the identities below are fixed checks.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
print("|e^(i pi) + 1| < 1e-12:", abs(exp(i * pi) + 1) < 1e-12)
print("real part of e^(i pi):", re(exp(i * pi)))
print("|e^i - (cos 1 + i sin 1)| < 1e-12:", abs(exp(i) - (cos(1) + i * sin(1))) < 1e-12)
/* ---- expected output ----
= |e^(i pi) + 1| < 1e-12: true
= real part of e^(i pi): -1
= |e^i - (cos 1 + i sin 1)| < 1e-12: true
*/
